// Conformance (currently PASSES against baseline, NOT because the bug is
// fixed -- see below): LEDGER item 3, 9th/10th investigation. Sequential
// (ZERO threads, zero spawn) SpinLock lock/unlock. This is the minimal
// repro that falsified the item's original "spawn/container sharing"
// attribution: a naive one-line fix to consume_call_args (add
// MirType::Struct to the Str/Array/Map borrow allowlist, WITHOUT the
// paired Cranelift retain at Terminator::Return + owner-count-checked
// struct release) crashed this 5/5 on JIT with "sync error: lock on
// dropped mutex" -- deterministically, with no concurrency involved at
// all. AOT was always clean for this exact repro (LLVM never boxes a
// plain struct; nothing to double-free at the struct level for an
// all-scalar struct like SpinLock/AtomicInt/Mutex).
//
// Root cause: SpinLock.lock() returns self bare. Cranelift boxes every
// struct value uniformly (top-level included), so the caller's lock local
// and the destination l end up as two independent Kryos-level owners of
// the SAME box with no retain anywhere -- each believes itself sole owner;
// whichever drops first frees a box the other still reads.
//
// 10th investigation (2026-08-28): implemented the fix described above
// (Cranelift retain at Terminator::Return for a bare struct-param return
// + owner-count-checked struct release) and proved it closes THIS repro
// clean, 5/5, both backends. REVERTED anyway, together with the rest of
// that session's changes -- read tools/loop/LEDGER.md item 3 in full
// before re-attempting: the same session found at least three MORE
// distinct struct-aliasing shapes the fix for THIS one does not cover (a
// struct param plainly rebound to a new local before being returned; the
// same self-return shape but with heap fields, LLVM-only; a struct read
// out of an array into a named local), the last discovered only after the
// first three were fixed and self-host-parser-shaped regression testing
// caught a NEW double-free. This file's own PASS today only means the
// compiler still has the (safe, leaking) baseline behavior -- it is
// pinned here so an 11th investigation has an immediate, isolated,
// already-diagnosed repro for mechanism #1 specifically, with no need to
// re-derive it.
use std::sync::{spin_lock}
fn main() {
    let lock = spin_lock()
    let mut i = 0
    while i < 5 {
        let l = lock.lock()
        l.unlock()
        i = i + 1
    }
    println("conformance spinlock sequential: PASS")
}
